---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import data from '../data/taz-bach/citations.json';

const pageTitle = 'Does the Taz Mostly Disagree with the Bach?';
const pageDescription = 'Testing Rav Asher Weiss’s remark that most of the time the Taz quotes the Bach, he disagrees with him — by classifying all 502 places the Taz cites his father-in-law in the full text, with an independent check by TypeSafe’s Jev.';

const { results, citations } = data;
const mine = results.mine;
const jev = results.jev;

const verdictLabel = { disagree: 'Disagrees', agree: 'Agrees', neutral: 'Neutral' };
const verdictClass = {
  disagree: 'bg-red-100 text-red-900 dark:bg-red-900/40 dark:text-red-100',
  agree: 'bg-green-100 text-green-900 dark:bg-green-900/40 dark:text-green-100',
  neutral: 'bg-stone-200 text-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-700 dark:text-stone-200',
};
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <main class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-widest opacity-60 mb-2">A full count — every citation · 8 November 2026</p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">Does the Taz Mostly Disagree with the Bach?</h1>
      <p class="text-lg opacity-80 max-w-3xl">
        In his shiur on <em>Parshat Toldot: Indirectly Causing an Obstacle</em>, Rav Asher Weiss
        said of the Taz and the Bach — father-in-law and son-in-law — “I didn't check it out
        statistically, but I think most of the times the Taz quotes the Bach, he disagrees with
        him.” He left the count open — this page runs it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm opacity-70">
        The shiur:
        <a class="underline hover:no-underline" href="https://yutorah-rss.netlify.app/transcripts/#265883@00:08:28" target="_blank" rel="noopener">transcript, 8:28</a>
        · <a class="underline hover:no-underline" href="https://www.torahanytime.com/lectures/265883" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TorahAnytime lecture 265883</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mb-10 rounded-xl border border-current/20 p-5 md:p-6">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-2">The verdict: yes, mostly — but by a narrow majority</h2>
      <p class="mb-3">
        Every one of the <strong>502</strong> places where the Taz cites the Bach was classified:
        he <strong>disagrees 236 times, agrees 185, and takes no position 81</strong>.
        Among the 421 citations where he takes a position at all, he disagrees
        <strong>56% of the time</strong>. That is a majority, not a landslide — and it is not a
        sample, so there is no sampling error to hide behind: the uncertainty here is
        classification judgment, not chance.
        TypeSafe's Jev, classifying the same 502 independently, is more disagree-leaning:
        <strong>271 disagree, 110 agree, 121 neutral — 71%</strong> among position-takers.
        The two methods bracket the answer; the human reading sits at the lower end.
      </p>
      <p>
        The pattern is not uniform. In <strong>Even HaEzer he disagrees in 62 of 76</strong>
        position-taking citations (82%). In <strong>Orach Chaim he agrees slightly more often
        than he disagrees</strong> (92 agree, 82 disagree). The “mostly disagrees” is real,
        and it lives mostly in Even HaEzer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">How it was counted</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal ml-6 space-y-2 max-w-3xl">
        <li>
          <strong>Source.</strong> The full Hebrew text of the Taz (<em>Turei Zahav</em>) on all
          four parts of Shulchan Arukh, from Sefaria — 1,705 simanim, fetched siman by siman.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Finding the Bach.</strong> There are 794 references to the Bach in the Taz.
          The trap: the abbreviation <span dir="rtl" lang="he">ב״ח</span> usually does
          <em>not</em> mean the Bach — in the Taz it is mostly <span dir="rtl" lang="he">בעל חוב</span> (a
          creditor), and also <span dir="rtl" lang="he">בבא בתרא</span>, <span dir="rtl" lang="he">ברכת חתנים</span>
          and <span dir="rtl" lang="he">בן חורין</span>. Only about 30% of
          <span dir="rtl" lang="he">ב״ח</span> tokens are the Bach at all, and they cannot be told
          apart without reading each one — so they are not in this count. The clean population is
          <span dir="rtl" lang="he">מו״ח</span> — “my teacher, my father-in-law” — which is
          unambiguously the Bach: <strong>499 occurrences</strong>, plus 6 standalone
          <span dir="rtl" lang="he">חמי</span> and the single spelled-out
          <span dir="rtl" lang="he">בית חדש</span> — 506 candidates. Two were excluded on inspection:
          <span dir="rtl" lang="he">רמי בר חמי</span> (Rami bar Chama, not “my father-in-law”) and a
          <span dir="rtl" lang="he">חמי זקיני הט״ז</span> that is a later glossator's voice, not the
          Taz's. Final population: <strong>502</strong> (Orach Chaim 218, Yoreh De'ah 193,
          Even HaEzer 89, Choshen Mishpat 2).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Census, not a sample.</strong> All 502 citations were read in context and
          classified <em>disagree / agree / neutral</em> — 60 by hand, the other 442 by nine
          classifiers working to the same rubric, each reading the full seif where the passage
          did not settle it. “Neutral” covers citations where the Taz reports the Bach, and cases
          where he answers the Bach's difficulty without ruling on the Bach's position. Later
          editor glosses (<span dir="rtl" lang="he">דברי המגיה</span>) were excluded. Sixteen
          citations are marked uncertain even after a full-seif read.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Independent check.</strong> Jev (jev-1.14.0) classified all 502 passages.
          It agreed on 315 of 502 (63%) — see below.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Earlier sample.</strong> A first pass classified a random sample of 60 before the
          full count was commissioned: 30 disagree, 20 agree, 10 neutral — 60% among
          position-takers. The census comes in a little lower (56%), as a full count should.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">By section</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-current/20">
              <th class="py-2 pr-4">Section</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-4">Citations</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-4">Disagree</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-4">Agree</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-4">Neutral</th>
              <th class="py-2">Disagree, of those taking a position</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-b border-current/10"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Orach Chaim</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">218</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">82</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">92</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">44</td><td class="py-2">47%</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-current/10"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Yoreh De'ah</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">193</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">91</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">78</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">24</td><td class="py-2">54%</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-current/10"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Even HaEzer</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">89</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">62</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">14</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">13</td><td class="py-2">82%</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-current/10"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Choshen Mishpat</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">2</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">0</td><td class="py-2">50%</td></tr>
            <tr class="font-bold"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Total</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">502</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">236</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">185</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">81</td><td class="py-2">56%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm opacity-70 max-w-3xl">
        Choshen Mishpat has only 2 unambiguous citations in the whole Taz — the Bach is cited
        there almost entirely as <span dir="rtl" lang="he">ב״ח</span>, where he is indistinguishable
        from a creditor without reading.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Three moments that show the range</h2>
      <div class="space-y-5 max-w-3xl">
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold">The bluntest disagreement — Yoreh De'ah 98:7</p>
          <p>
            The one place the Bach is spelled out in full — <span dir="rtl" lang="he">בספר בית חדש פסק מו״ח ז״ל</span> —
            he rules leniently, that after the fact the food is permitted. The Taz answers:
            <span dir="rtl" lang="he">ולע״ד נראה דלא להקל כלל בזה</span> — “in my humble opinion, one should
            not be lenient in this at all.”
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold">The sharpest language — Even HaEzer 129:25</p>
          <p>
            On the Bach's ruling about a get: <span dir="rtl" lang="he">מכשול יצא מתחת ידי מו״ח ז״ל בזה</span> —
            “a stumbling block came from under the Bach's hand in this.” The Taz says the Bach
            himself was not happy with it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold">The reverse — Yoreh De'ah 204:5</p>
          <p>
            The Taz presented his reading to the Bach in person after the Bach had printed his
            work — <span dir="rtl" lang="he">והסכים לדברי ואמר בפירוש שרמ״א טעה בכך</span> — “and he agreed
            with my words, and said explicitly that the Rema had erred in this.” Sometimes the
            father-in-law conceded.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold">The shiur's own case — Yoreh De'ah 147</p>
          <p>
            The dispute Weiss was teaching — whether a non-Jew may say thank you to idolatry —
            leaves no named trace: the Taz's comment on Yoreh De'ah 147 never mentions the Bach
            at all. The disagreement there is substantive, not a citation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">The Jev check — same direction, further apart</h2>
      <p class="max-w-3xl mb-3">
        Jev classified all 502 passages: 271 disagree, 110 agree, 121 neutral — 71% disagree
        among position-takers, against the human reading's 56%. It agreed with the human verdict
        on 315 of 502 (63%). The gap is systematic: Jev rarely calls a citation an agreement.
        Of the 185 citations the human reading calls agree, Jev agreed on only 77 — it called
        56 of them disagree and 52 neutral. And it misreads characteristic Taz idiom:
        <span dir="rtl" lang="he">יפה הקשה</span> (“he asked well” — praise, not a fight),
        <span dir="rtl" lang="he">ובחנם</span> (“for nothing” — a dismissal),
        <span dir="rtl" lang="he">ולא כדעת</span> (“not like the view of” — an explicit disagreement).
        The two methods agree on the direction and the section pattern — Jev too puts Even
        HaEzer highest (58 of 65 position-takers, 89%) — but the exact disagree share depends
        on the reader.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Caveats</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc ml-6 space-y-2 max-w-3xl">
        <li>
          This is a census, so there is no sampling error — but classification is judgment.
          Sixteen citations are marked uncertain even after a full-seif read, and the Jev check
          lands 15 points higher on the disagree share. Treat 56% as the human reading, with Jev's
          71% as the upper bracket.
        </li>
        <li>
          Each verdict rests on the passage around the citation, extended to the full seif where
          needed. Longer context — the Bach's own text, or the sugya — could flip a few.
          The contested ones are marked in the table where Jev and the human reading differ.
        </li>
        <li>
          “Disagree” covers a range, from the blunt <span dir="rtl" lang="he">דזה אינו</span> (“this is
          not so”) to the mild <span dir="rtl" lang="he">בחנם</span> (“for nothing” — the Bach's
          move was unnecessary) and <span dir="rtl" lang="he">לא נהירא</span> (“it is not clear to me”).
        </li>
        <li>
          The Taz often answers the Bach's difficulty rather than ruling on it; those are counted
          neutral, which is why the disagree share is higher among position-takers (56%) than
          across all citations (47%).
        </li>
        <li>
          The ~119 <span dir="rtl" lang="he">ב״ח</span> tokens that look like the Bach are not in
          this count: each would need to be read individually to separate the Bach from creditors
          and the other abbreviations. They would move the totals, not the method.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-3">All 502 citations</h2>
      <div class="mb-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2 text-sm" id="filters">
        <button class="filter-btn px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-current/30" data-filter="all">All · 502</button>
        <button class="filter-btn px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-current/30" data-filter="disagree">Disagree · 236</button>
        <button class="filter-btn px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-current/30" data-filter="agree">Agree · 185</button>
        <button class="filter-btn px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-current/30" data-filter="neutral">Neutral · 81</button>
        <button class="filter-btn px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-current/30" data-filter="differ">Muse–Jev differ · 187</button>
      </div>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse text-sm" id="citeTable">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-current/20">
              <th class="py-2 pr-3">#</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-3">Source</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-3">Passage</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-3">Taz vs Bach</th>
              <th class="py-2">Jev</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {citations.map((c) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-current/10 align-top cite-row" data-verdict={c.mine} data-differ={String(!c.agree)}>
                <td class="py-2 pr-3 whitespace-nowrap opacity-70">{c.id}</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-3 whitespace-nowrap">
                  <a class="underline hover:no-underline" href={c.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{c.ref}</a>
                </td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-3" dir="rtl" lang="he">{c.hebrew}</td>
                <td class="py-2 pr-3 whitespace-nowrap">
                  <span class={`px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold ${verdictClass[c.mine]}`}>{verdictLabel[c.mine]}</span>
                </td>
                <td class="py-2 whitespace-nowrap">
                  <span class={`px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold ${verdictClass[c.jev]}`}>{verdictLabel[c.jev]}</span>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-6 text-sm opacity-70 max-w-3xl">
      <h2 class="text-base font-bold mb-2 opacity-100">Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Taz text: Sefaria, <em>Turei Zahav on Shulchan Arukh</em> (Ashlei Ravrevei, Lemberg 1888).
        The claim: Rav Asher Weiss, “Parshat Toldot: Indirectly Causing an Obstacle,” 16 November 2023,
        at 8:28 — <a class="underline hover:no-underline" href="https://yutorah-rss.netlify.app/transcripts/#265883@00:08:28" target="_blank" rel="noopener">transcript</a>.
        Classification: all 502 unambiguous Bach citations in the Taz (499
        <span dir="rtl" lang="he">מו״ח</span>, 6 <span dir="rtl" lang="he">חמי</span>, 1 spelled-out
        <span dir="rtl" lang="he">בית חדש</span>, minus 2 false positives), read in context;
        Jev (jev-1.14.0) classified the same 502 independently.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <script>
    const btns = document.querySelectorAll('.filter-btn');
    const rows = document.querySelectorAll('.cite-row');
    btns.forEach((btn) => {
      btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const f = btn.getAttribute('data-filter');
        rows.forEach((row) => {
          let show = true;
          if (f === 'differ') show = row.getAttribute('data-differ') === 'true';
          else if (f !== 'all') show = row.getAttribute('data-verdict') === f;
          row.style.display = show ? '' : 'none';
        });
        btns.forEach((b) => b.classList.remove('opacity-100', 'font-bold'));
        btn.classList.add('opacity-100', 'font-bold');
      });
    });
  </script>
</Layout>
